; pi4SlowSpin.pi4 - looks like a hang, but gives up on its own.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS INSTEAD OF pi4Hang.pi4. Proving the deadman works
; means running something the deadman has to rescue. pi4Hang.pi4 does
; that honestly and has one bad property: if the deadman DOESN'T work,
; the board is wedged until a human pulls the power. That is a fine
; trade with someone sitting at the bench and a poor one at 3 a.m.
;
; So this spins for a fixed wall-clock time and then returns normally.
; Armed with a SHORTER deadman, the two outcomes are distinguishable and
; only one of them can go wrong:
;
;   deadman fires   -> the board resets partway through. PROVEN.
;   deadman broken  -> this returns to the prompt. Board still healthy,
;                      and the failure is reported rather than wedging.
;
; A test whose failure mode is "everything is fine, and you know" is
; worth more than a purer one that has to be supervised.
;
; The wait is on CNTPCT_EL0 via timer.pi4, not a spin count, so it is
; the same ten seconds at 600 MHz and at 1.5 GHz.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile pi4SlowSpin.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x400000 \
;        --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns -o slow.img

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"

Procedure.i Main()
  Define i.i

  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("=== spinning for 10 s. a shorter deadman should cut this short. ===")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; One line a second, so the transcript shows exactly WHEN it stopped
  ; rather than only that it did. If the last number printed is 2 and a
  ; U-Boot banner follows, the deadman fired at three seconds and the
  ; evidence is on the page.
  For i = 1 To 10
    Delay(1000)
    UartWriteStr("  t+")
    PrintDec(i)
    UartWriteStr(" s")
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
  Next

  UartWriteStr("=== reached the end. THE DEADMAN DID NOT FIRE. ===")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
  ProcedureReturn 10
EndProcedure
